    "recommendations": "Reducing brush immediately adjacent to the structure and creating greater separation between dense and continuous shrub cover and the building perimeter would improve defensible space and reduce fuel continuity near improvements. Where shrubs directly contact or closely border the structure, thinning and clearing that vegetation could meaningfully reduce direct flame and radiant heat exposure to the building. Managing vegetation along the single access route is particularly important, as maintaining adequate width, vertical clearance, and reduced fuel concentrations along this corridor would support firefighter access and safe evacuation. Given the property's wildland interface setting and open terrain, preserving and expanding any irrigated landscaping near the structure as a fuel break may further reduce opportunities for fire spread. A sustained vegetation management program addressing the surrounding shrub cover within the immediate structure zone would support long-term wildfire resilience and improve overall property preparedness."
